---
title: "[REDACTED] Camera: Blur Faces on Video Calls (Mac)"
description: A free virtual camera for Mac that blurs the faces behind you on Zoom, Google Meet, Teams and FaceTime calls. Face detection runs on your Mac. Video never leaves your computer.
---
<% app = data.apps.find { |a| a.slug == "redacted-camera" } %>

<section class="tool-hero app-hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <%= partial "partials/brand" %>
    <h1>[REDACTED] Camera</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      A virtual camera for Mac that blurs faces in real time.
      Show your face on a video call and hide the people behind you.
      Works with Zoom, Google Meet, Teams, FaceTime and any other video app.
    </p>
    <div class="app-download">
      <a href="/downloads/<%= app.file %>" class="button button-big" download>Download for Mac</a>
      <p class="app-meta">Free &middot; <%= app.platform %> &middot; Apple silicon and Intel &middot; Version <%= app.version %> &middot; <%= app.download_size %></p>
    </div>
    <figure class="app-shot">
      <img src="<%= image_path "apps/redacted-camera-hero.webp" %>" width="1672" height="941"
           alt="A woman on a video call in a busy café. Her face is clear. The faces of three people behind her are blurred.">
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="app-section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="app-split">
      <div class="app-copy">
        <h2>Click a face to show or hide it</h2>
        <p>[REDACTED] Camera finds every face in your webcam video and hides it. To show your own face, click it in the app. Click it again to hide it.</p>
        <p>A person who walks into the picture is always hidden, so you can take a call in a café, a shared office or at home without showing anyone who did not agree to be on camera.</p>
        <p>Choose Blur, Pixelate or Box. The blur follows each face as it moves, and grows when a head turns, to cover ears and hair.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="app-windows">
        <figure class="mac-window">
          <div class="mac-titlebar" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <img src="<%= image_path "apps/redacted-camera-app-all-hidden.webp" %>" width="970" height="658"
               alt="The [REDACTED] Camera app with all four faces in the café blurred and marked Hidden.">
          <figcaption>All faces hidden</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <figure class="mac-window">
          <div class="mac-titlebar" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></div>
          <img src="<%= image_path "apps/redacted-camera-app-you-shown.webp" %>" width="970" height="658"
               alt="The same video after one click: the woman's face is shown and the three faces behind her stay blurred.">
          <figcaption>One click: your face shown</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="app-section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Works with every video app</h2>
    <p class="app-intro">The app adds a camera called <strong>[REDACTED] Camera</strong> to your Mac. Keep the app open and choose that camera in your video app.</p>
    <div class="notes app-notes">
      <div>
        <h3>Blur Face on Zoom Call</h3>
        <p>In Zoom, open Settings &rarr; Video and choose [REDACTED] Camera. In a meeting, you can also click the arrow next to Stop Video and choose it there.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Blur Face on Google Meet</h3>
        <p>In a Meet call, click More options &rarr; Settings &rarr; Video and choose [REDACTED] Camera. If it is not in the list, reload the page after you open the app.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Blur Face on Microsoft Teams</h3>
        <p>In Teams, open Settings &rarr; Devices and choose [REDACTED] Camera under Camera. Before you join a meeting, you can also pick it in the camera menu.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Blur Face on FaceTime</h3>
        <p>In a FaceTime call, open the Video menu in the menu bar and choose [REDACTED] Camera.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Blur Face on Webex</h3>
        <p>In a Webex meeting, click the arrow next to Stop Video and choose [REDACTED] Camera.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Blur Face on Slack Huddles</h3>
        <p>In Slack, open Settings &rarr; Audio &amp; video and choose [REDACTED] Camera under Camera. Your next huddle with video uses it.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Blur Face on Discord</h3>
        <p>In Discord, open User Settings &rarr; Voice &amp; Video and choose [REDACTED] Camera under Camera.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Blur Face on Any Other App</h3>
        <p>Any Mac app that lets you choose a camera can use [REDACTED] Camera, including OBS, Loom and Photo Booth.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="app-section">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Install</h2>
    <ol class="app-steps">
      <li><a href="/downloads/<%= app.file %>" download>Download the app</a>, open the file and drag [REDACTED] Camera to Applications.</li>
      <li>Open [REDACTED] Camera and allow it to use your camera.</li>
      <li>Click Install. macOS asks you to allow the camera extension: click Open System Settings and turn on [REDACTED] Camera under Camera Extensions.</li>
      <li>In your video app, choose [REDACTED] Camera as your camera.</li>
    </ol>

    <div class="notes">
      <div>
        <h3>Private by design</h3>
        <p>Face detection uses Apple's Vision framework on your Mac. The app has no network code and no account. Your video goes only to the apps that you choose.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Low CPU use</h3>
        <p>Click Pause Camera to turn off your webcam and the face detection. Video apps then show a message in place of your video.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Check before you rely on it</h3>
        <p>Very small, far away or covered faces can be missed. Look at the preview in the app before a call.</p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Remove it at any time</h3>
        <p>Open the app's Settings and click Uninstall to remove the camera. Then drag the app to the Trash.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
